/// Ownership: the marker that makes a type responsible for something it must release.
///
/// A type implementing `Drop` becomes move-only, and so does any aggregate holding one, because a value with a
/// destructor cannot be duplicated by copying its bytes. Passing, returning, initializing or assigning one consumes
/// it, and reading it afterwards is rejected with a use-after-move diagnostic. That much is enforced today, and it is
/// what makes `Drop` worth implementing now: it is how a type says that its value has one owner at a time.
///
/// Implement it with `extend MyType : Drop`, which is what marks the type droppable; declaring a method named `Drop`
/// without the interface does not.
///
/// # Safety
///
/// **The compiler does not yet run `Drop`.** It decides where destruction belongs — ordinary scope exit, return,
/// `break` and `continue`, the value an assignment overwrites, `?` propagation, and the parts already built when
/// constructing an aggregate fails — and it records a plan for each of those points, but no call is emitted for them
/// yet. A type that must release something has to release it explicitly until that lands. Do not write a `Drop` body
/// that a program's correctness depends on having run.
///
/// When emission arrives, `Drop` will run once per value that was not moved away, before the type's fields and
/// elements are released in reverse construction order. Panic and process termination will not unwind, so it will not
/// run on those paths. A resource whose release can fail should therefore also offer an explicit `Close` that
/// consumes the value and reports the error; `Drop` has no way to report one.
///
/// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/drop
interface Drop {
    /// Releases whatever this value owns.
    ///
    /// Borrows the value through a `*var` receiver rather than consuming it: the storage is still there and the
    /// compiler reclaims it once this returns.
    ///
    /// Must not fail and must not panic, because there is no caller to report to and no way to stop the destruction
    /// already under way.
    ///
    /// # Safety
    ///
    /// Not called yet. See the note on the interface.
    ///
    /// https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/core/drop/drop
    func Drop();
}
